    "Beyond the forecast, Nyx knows how cloudy each park's nights usually are in each month: ten years (2015 to 2024) of ERA5, a record of past weather rebuilt from observations, averaged over each night's true darkness at the park's coordinates. Nights without a forecast are scored with these usual clouds and say so (\"No cloud forecast yet\"), so a park whose winter nights are mostly overcast does not tie a desert and a night nobody can forecast is never treated as clear. They also say how often that month's nights are mostly clear (under 30% cloud). It is a long-term average, never a forecast. Generated using Copernicus Climate Change Service information [2015–2024]: ERA5 (Hersbach et al. 2020; DOI 10.24381/cds.adbb2d47), read from its hourly time series (DOI 10.24381/1cf1ad76) and averaged over each night's true darkness by Nyx. Licensed under CC BY 4.0. Neither the European Commission nor ECMWF is responsible for any use that may be made of the Copernicus information or data it contains.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Beyond the forecast, Nyx knows how cloudy each park's nights usually are in each month: ten years (2015 to 2024) of ERA5, a record of past weather rebuilt from observations, averaged over each night's true darkness at the park's coordinates. Nights without a forecast are scored with these usual clouds and say so (\"No cloud forecast yet\"), so a park whose winter nights are mostly overcast does not tie a desert and a night nobody can forecast is never treated as clear. They also say how often that month's nights are mostly clear (under 30% cloud). It is a long-term average, never a forecast. Generated using Copernicus Climate Change Service information [2015–2024]: ERA5 (Hersbach et al. 2020; DOI 10.24381/cds.adbb2d47), read from its hourly time series (DOI 10.24381/1cf1ad76) and averaged over each night's true darkness by Nyx. Licensed under CC BY 4.0. Neither the European Commission nor ECMWF is responsible for any use that may be made of the Copernicus information or data it contains."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Más allá del pronóstico, Nyx sabe cuán nubladas suelen ser las noches de cada parque en cada mes: diez años (2015 a 2024) de ERA5, un registro del clima pasado reconstruido a partir de observaciones, promediados sobre la oscuridad total de cada noche en las coordenadas del parque. Las noches sin pronóstico se calculan con estas nubes habituales y lo indican (“Aún sin pronóstico de nubes”), para que un parque cuyas noches de invierno suelen estar cubiertas no empate con un desierto y una noche que nadie puede pronosticar nunca se trate como despejada. También indican con qué frecuencia las noches de ese mes están mayormente despejadas (menos del 30% de nubes). Es un promedio a largo plazo, nunca un pronóstico. Generado con información del Servicio de Cambio Climático de Copernicus [2015–2024]: ERA5 (Hersbach et al. 2020; DOI 10.24381/cds.adbb2d47), leída de su serie horaria (DOI 10.24381/1cf1ad76) y promediada por Nyx sobre la oscuridad total de cada noche. Con licencia CC BY 4.0. Ni la Comisión Europea ni el ECMWF son responsables del uso que pueda hacerse de la información o los datos de Copernicus que contiene."
          }
        }
      }
    },
