    "Light-pollution estimates use NASA Black Marble nighttime lights (VNP46A4, DOI 10.5067/VIIRS/VNP46A4.002; Román et al. 2018), public domain. Nyx adds the 2025 yearly satellite light within 300 km of each park and viewing spot, weakening with distance (Walker's law), and compares the result across the 63 parks. The same sum, split by direction, finds the light domes on the horizon; a town is named only when a known one lies near the light. It is a comparison between places, not a measurement of sky brightness: it models no terrain, haze or light color, cannot resolve a lit lodge next to a spot, and cannot tell the darkest skies apart. The satellite misses much of the blue light of white LEDs, so towns that switched to LEDs can look darker than they are. The score keeps the conservative Bortle estimate; the satellite view is context. Growth since 2013 is shown only as a comparison between parks, and not for Alaska, lava, oil-field flaring or almost unlit places, where the change is not light pollution.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Light-pollution estimates use NASA Black Marble nighttime lights (VNP46A4, DOI 10.5067/VIIRS/VNP46A4.002; Román et al. 2018), public domain. Nyx adds the 2025 yearly satellite light within 300 km of each park and viewing spot, weakening with distance (Walker's law), and compares the result across the 63 parks. The same sum, split by direction, finds the light domes on the horizon; a town is named only when a known one lies near the light. It is a comparison between places, not a measurement of sky brightness: it models no terrain, haze or light color, cannot resolve a lit lodge next to a spot, and cannot tell the darkest skies apart. The satellite misses much of the blue light of white LEDs, so towns that switched to LEDs can look darker than they are. The score keeps the conservative Bortle estimate; the satellite view is context. Growth since 2013 is shown only as a comparison between parks, and not for Alaska, lava, oil-field flaring or almost unlit places, where the change is not light pollution."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Las estimaciones de contaminación lumínica usan las luces nocturnas de NASA Black Marble (VNP46A4, DOI 10.5067/VIIRS/VNP46A4.002; Román et al. 2018), de dominio público. Nyx suma la luz satelital anual de 2025 en un radio de 300 km de cada parque y punto de observación, atenuada con la distancia (ley de Walker), y compara el resultado entre los 63 parques. La misma suma, dividida por dirección, encuentra los domos de luz en el horizonte; solo se nombra una población cuando hay una conocida cerca de la luz. Es una comparación entre lugares, no una medición del brillo del cielo: no modela el terreno, la bruma ni el color de la luz, no distingue un albergue iluminado junto a un punto de observación y no puede diferenciar los cielos más oscuros entre sí. El satélite no capta buena parte de la luz azul de los LED blancos, así que las poblaciones que cambiaron a LED pueden parecer más oscuras de lo que son. El índice conserva la estimación Bortle conservadora; la vista satelital es contexto. El crecimiento desde 2013 solo se muestra como comparación entre parques, y no para Alaska, zonas de lava, quema de gas en campos petroleros ni lugares casi sin luz, donde el cambio no es contaminación lumínica."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "Links to nps.gov pages, Recreation.gov campground reservations and Globe at Night open in Safari when you tap them. Nyx itself sends nothing to those sites.": {
