    "Open-Meteo forecasts cover up to 16 days. All 63 parks share each request, which carries only the public coordinates of each park's main viewing spot. Clouds are averaged over the complete window of true darkness; on nights without it, over sunset to sunrise, or the four hours around the Sun's lowest point under the midnight sun. A forecast counts in full up to about three days ahead of a night. From there to ten days it is eased toward the park's usual clouds for the month, and these nights are marked as an early look; past ten days, or with no forecast, the usual clouds count alone. An old forecast is never dropped; it simply counts for less, so going offline cannot make a night look clearer than its clouds allow. A forecast with incomplete coverage of a night is not used. At Haleakalā, whose summit sits above the trade-wind inversion, the score counts mid and high cloud when that forecast is available. Open-Meteo's data is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Nyx changes it: each night's clouds are averaged over that night's true darkness and eased toward the usual clouds, as described here.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Open-Meteo forecasts cover up to 16 days. All 63 parks share each request, which carries only the public coordinates of each park's main viewing spot. Clouds are averaged over the complete window of true darkness; on nights without it, over sunset to sunrise, or the four hours around the Sun's lowest point under the midnight sun. A forecast counts in full up to about three days ahead of a night. From there to ten days it is eased toward the park's usual clouds for the month, and these nights are marked as an early look; past ten days, or with no forecast, the usual clouds count alone. An old forecast is never dropped; it simply counts for less, so going offline cannot make a night look clearer than its clouds allow. A forecast with incomplete coverage of a night is not used. At Haleakalā, whose summit sits above the trade-wind inversion, the score counts mid and high cloud when that forecast is available. Open-Meteo's data is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Nyx changes it: each night's clouds are averaged over that night's true darkness and eased toward the usual clouds, as described here."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Los pronósticos de Open-Meteo cubren hasta 16 días. Los 63 parques comparten cada solicitud, que lleva solo las coordenadas públicas del punto de observación principal de cada parque. Las nubes se promedian sobre todo el periodo de oscuridad total; en noches sin ella, de la puesta a la salida del sol, o en las cuatro horas alrededor del punto más bajo del Sol bajo el sol de medianoche. Un pronóstico cuenta por completo hasta unos tres días antes de una noche. De ahí hasta diez días se ajusta hacia la nubosidad habitual del parque en ese mes, y esas noches se marcan como vista anticipada; pasados diez días, o sin pronóstico, cuenta solo la nubosidad habitual. Un pronóstico viejo nunca se descarta; simplemente cuenta menos, así que quedarte sin conexión no puede hacer que una noche se vea más despejada de lo que permiten sus nubes. Un pronóstico con cobertura incompleta de una noche no se usa. En Haleakalā, cuya cumbre está por encima de la inversión de los vientos alisios, el índice cuenta las nubes medias y altas cuando ese pronóstico está disponible. Los datos de Open-Meteo tienen licencia CC BY 4.0. Nyx los modifica: las nubes de cada noche se promedian sobre la oscuridad total de esa noche y se ajustan hacia la nubosidad habitual, como se describe aquí."
          }
        }
      }
    },
